     274→## Extend
     275→You can simply extend debugger 
     276→```js
     277→const log = require('debug')('auth');
     278→
     279→//creates new debug instance with extended namespace
     280→const logSign = log.extend('sign');
     281→const logLogin = log.extend('login');
     282→
     283→log('hello'); // auth hello
     284→logSign('hello'); //auth:sign hello
     285→logLogin('hello'); //auth:login hello
     286→```
     288→## Set dynamically
    …
     341→## Checking whether a debug target is enabled
     342→
     343→After you've created a debug instance, you can determine whether or not it is
     344→enabled by checking the `enabled` property:
     345→
     346→```javascript
     347→const debug = require('debug')('http');
     348→
     349→if (debug.enabled) {
     350→  // do stuff...
     351→}
     352→```
     353→
     354→You can also manually toggle this property to force the debug instance to be
     355→enabled or disabled.
     357→## Usage in child processes
     358→
     359→Due to the way `debug` detects if the output is a TTY or not, colors are not shown in child processes when `stderr` is piped. A solution is to pass the `DEBUG_COLORS=1` environment variable to the child process.  
     360→For example:
     361→
     362→```javascript
     363→worker = fork(WORKER_WRAP_PATH, [workerPath], {
     364→  stdio: [
     365→    /* stdin: */ 0,
     366→    /* stdout: */ 'pipe',
     367→    /* stderr: */ 'pipe',
     368→    'ipc',
     369→  ],
     370→  env: Object.assign({}, process.env, {
     371→    DEBUG_COLORS: 1 // without this settings, colors won't be shown
     372→  }),
     373→});
     374→
     375→worker.stderr.pipe(process.stderr, { end: false });
     376→```
